  "answer_text": "Calcium is an essential mineral that helps build and maintain strong bones and teeth, assists with muscle function, and supports nerve signaling. \n\nThe Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) for most adults is **1,000 mg per day**, which increases to **1,200 mg per day** for women over 50 and men over 70.\n\nHere is a breakdown of the best food sources of calcium, categorized by food group:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Dairy Products\nDairy is traditionally the most well-known and highly bioavailable source of calcium.\n*   **Yogurt (plain, low-fat):** ~300\u2013450 mg per cup\n*   **Milk (whole, low-fat, or skim):** ~300 mg per cup\n*   **Mozzarella cheese (part-skim):** ~200 mg per ounce (or ~600 mg per cup)\n*   **Cheddar cheese:** ~200 mg per ounce\n\n### 2. Plant-Based & Non-Dairy Milks (Fortified)\nIf you are lactose intolerant, vegan, or simply prefer non-dairy options, fortified foods are highly effective sources of calcium.\n*   **Fortified Almond Milk:** ~440 mg per cup\n*   **Fortified Orange Juice:** ~350 mg per cup\n*   **Fortified Soy Milk:** ~300\u2013450 mg per cup\n*   **Fortified Rice Milk:** ~280 mg per cup\n\n### 3. Vegetables (Dark Leafy Greens)\nMany dark, leafy greens are excellent sources of calcium, though their absorption rate varies depending on how much oxalate (a compound that can bind to calcium) they contain. Low-oxalate greens like bok choy and turnip greens are absorbed very well.\n*   **Turnip greens (cooked):** ~204 mg per cup\n*   **Bok choy (cooked):** ~197 mg per cup\n*   **Kale (cooked):** ~184 mg per cup\n*   **Mustard greens (cooked):** ~165 mg per cup\n*   **Broccoli (steamed):** ~90 mg per cup\n\n### 4. Soy & Legumes\n*   **Tofu (prepared with calcium sulfate):** ~434 mg per 1/2 cup *(Check the label to ensure \"calcium sulfate\" or \"calcium chloride\" was used as a coagulant).*\n*   **Edamame (cooked):** ~100 mg per cup\n\n### 5. Seafood (with Soft Bones)\nSmall fish eaten with their soft, edible bones are incredibly rich in calcium.\n*   **Canned Sardines (with bones):** ~325 mg per 3 ounces\n*   **Canned Pink Salmon (with bones):** ~180 mg per 3 ounces\n\n### 6. Nuts, Seeds, & Grains\n*   **Tahini (sesame paste):** ~154 mg per tablespoon\n*   **Chia seeds:** ~180 mg per 2 tablespoons\n*   **Almonds:** ~75 mg per ounce (about 23 almonds)\n*   **Fortified Breakfast Cereals:** Up to 100 to 1,000 mg per serving (depending on the brand)\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udca1 Tips for Maximum Calcium Absorption\n*   **Get enough Vitamin D:** Your body cannot absorb calcium efficiently without Vitamin D. Ensure you get regular sunlight exposure or consume Vitamin D-rich foods (like egg yolks, fatty fish, and fortified foods).\n*   **Spread it out:** Your body absorbs calcium best in doses of **500 mg or less** at a time. Rather than trying to get all your daily calcium in one meal, space your intake throughout the day.\n*   **Limit caffeine and sodium:** High intakes of sodium and caffeine can increase the amount of calcium your body excretes in urine.",
